  "evidence": "TEXT_G: Detailed economy/tax plans (raise personal allowance to £11,500; merge income tax and NI; cut state size; split retail/investment banking), explicit job creation (one million skilled jobs via national programmes), NHS commitments (no frontline cuts; elected boards; vouchers), schools and welfare reform, and a managed immigration freeze then cap with stronger borders; law and order (life means life; double prison places); transport, housing, and energy policies. TEXT_C: Heavy emphasis on halting immigration and identity measures (ban burka, ritual slaughter, new mosques; repeal Race Relations Act), immediate EU exit, punitive crime policies (corporal and capital punishment; hard labour), defence repositioning, and some economic measures (raise allowance to £12,500; tariffs; oppose privatisations; separate banking), plus NHS prioritised for Britons and nationalised telecoms.",
  "reasoning": "Voters’ top concerns were broadly economy/jobs, the NHS/public services, immigration and crime; TEXT_G provides comprehensive, mainstream-oriented commitments on tax, job creation, NHS and schools alongside controlled immigration. TEXT_C foregrounds identity/Islam/EU and punitive justice changes that are more niche, with less balanced focus on the economy and public services, so it aligns less with the electorate’s overall priorities.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_G"
}
